node -v to check what ver of node js you have
npm -v to check what ver of package manager you have
npm init -y to initate a new node project with package manager

when it comes to typescript:
node js can only compile javascript
so when it comes to .ts file, its purpose is to prevent bugs by defining strict types
Types tell your computer what type of value something like a variable can have

To use typescript and other validation tools for ts, you need to install them by doing
npm add --save-dev typescript tsx vitest @types/node

**reminder to make a gitignore for node_modules
we dont commit node_modules because it clogs the repo with unnecessary large files, it is bad practice
other tips:
never delete package-lock.json, it tracks your dependencies
don't modify node_modules
never commit your environment
be careful of installing globals as it won't be listed in package.json

Modules:
there are two modules, ESM(best for any new projects) and commonjs(best for legacy projects)
in ESM you must list import statements at the very top
npm pkg set type=module updates your package.json type from commonjs to esm

Tsconfig:
this is a file that is needed to mark the root directory of your typescript project and also acts as a configuration blueprint

When you have tsconfig.json, you can run tsc (the typescript compiler) in your terminal for your node js project
The compiler will read your tsconfig.json file, find your TypeScript files, and compile them all into JavaScript in one go.

You can generate tsconfig.json with npx tsc --init (included in node js)

Src:
where all your source code exists

Typescript:
ts interfaces are written as:
export interface ____ {

}
their purpose is to define strict types
you can use readonly to ensure that these types cannot be changed later

